From: Roman Rikhter <teledurak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: rtw88: take the MAC address from the device tree

A WLAN MAC in a SoC has no MAC address of its own; the board keeps it
in flash. Take the address from the device tree (a "mac-address" nvmem
cell or property) when there is one.

Signed-off-by: Roman Rikhter <teledurak@gmail.com>
---
diff --git drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/main.c drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/main.c
index fe1d65e..eb5d1cb 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/main.c
@@ -3,6 +3,7 @@
  */
 
 #include <linux/devcoredump.h>
+#include <linux/of_net.h>
 
 #include "main.h"
 #include "regd.h"
@@ -2060,6 +2061,9 @@ static int rtw_chip_efuse_info_setup(struct rtw_dev *rtwdev)
 	efuse->ext_pa_5g = efuse->pa_type_5g & BIT(0) ? 1 : 0;
 	efuse->ext_lna_5g = efuse->lna_type_5g & BIT(3) ? 1 : 0;
 
+	/* a MAC address from the device tree takes precedence */
+	of_get_mac_address(rtwdev->dev->of_node, efuse->addr);
+
 	if (!is_valid_ether_addr(efuse->addr)) {
 		eth_random_addr(efuse->addr);
 		dev_warn(rtwdev->dev, "efuse MAC invalid, using random\n");
